			"isLocked": false,
			"opacity": 1,
			"meta": {},
			"id": "shape:5E6EwVBZ4vhc6IOMH1BA5",
			"type": "text",
			"props": {
				"color": "black",
				"size": "s",
				"w": 1536.0235750173947,
				"font": "draw",
				"textAlign": "start",
				"autoSize": false,
				"scale": 1,
				"richText": {
					"type": "doc",
					"attrs": {
						"dir": "auto"
					},
					"content": [
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "docker ps command lists all the running containers and some basic information about them such as the container ID, the name of the image we use to run the container, the current status and the name of the container. Each container automatically gets a random ID"
								}
							]
						}
					]
				}
			},
			"parentId": "page:kevi_8ziZRBjXGF6dbUmV",
			"index": "aHYAM",
			"typeName": "shape"
		},
		{
			"x": 36.18199451637901,
			"y": 1171.78519290272,
			"rotation": 0,
			"isLocked": false,
			"opacity": 1,
			"meta": {},
			"id": "shape:ATXxDm6huwVvYtBSYaZFy",
			"type": "text",
			"props": {
				"color": "black",
				"size": "s",
				"w": 1340.1468577219384,
				"font": "draw",
				"textAlign": "start",
				"autoSize": false,
				"scale": 1.027153406582559,
				"richText": {
					"type": "doc",
					"attrs": {
						"dir": "auto"
					},
					"content": [
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Why do we need Docker?."
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Consider we are building an app and we are using windows machine, we are using multiple libraries like tailwind, storybook, Next.js and Reddis when you are developing this app your machine might be using node version 22 but the deployed version of your code might have 20 in this case the changes you made are working in your local but not in the deployed version or vice versa. In the same way the changes you made might not work in Mac machine. Docker will solve this problem of working in local and not working in prod and removes the underlying os dependency. "
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "What is a Container?."
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Containers are completely isolated environments. They can have their own processes or services, their own network interfaces, their own mounts, just like virtula machine, expect they all share the same OS Kernal."
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Note: Container are not new with Docker. Containers have existed for about more than 10 years and some of the different types of containers are LXC, LXD, LXCFS and so on"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Initially Docker was build on LXC back in the version 0.9.0 and that was around 2014 and switched to it's own library called libcontainer, libcontainer later become runc which is the reference implementation of OCI runtime specifications. SO, OCI stands for open container initiative and it's the open standard that defines how container runtimes and Image format should work. So, today when docker runs it's actually runs of containerd and runc not LXC, LXD, LXCFS"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "\tSetting up these low-level linux container environment is hard and that is where Docker offers a high level tool with several powerful functionalities, making it really easy for end users and that's how Docker became so popular."
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "How DOcker WOrks?."
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Kernal: A Kernal is the core part of an operating system. it acts as a bridge between software applications and the hardware of a computer"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "DOcker's underlying OS Kernal is a Linux Kernal"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "So the containers that are created by Docker are using the linux Kernal"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "There are total 3 types of popular operating systems available"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "  1. Windows"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "  2. Mac os"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "  3. Linux"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Docker can run on linux easier and faster since, docker natively runs or Linux."
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "On Windows or Mac OS Docker Desktop downloads a lightweight Linux VM behind the scenes and creates container on this VM(virtual machine)"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "So, it's linux container inside linux vm inside Windows."
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "\tContainers vs Virtual Machines"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "The fundamental difference is that the virtual Machines(VM's) virtualize physical hardware, while containers virtualize the operating systems kernal"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "What is a Hypervisor?."
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "A Hypervisor is software that creates and runs virtual machines by dividing one physical computer hardware into multiple isolated environments"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Since Virtual Machines are completed isolated from each other and VM's don't rely on the underlying OS or Kernal you can run different type of application build on different OS or Kernal windows based app and linux based app on the same hypervisor you can use a container inside the virtual Machine to power up many containers"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Image VS Container"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "There are lot of containerized version of applications readily available today most organizations have their product containerized and available in the public Docker Repository called Docker Hub. Docker Hub is the most popular registry but there are many others too like github containerized repository, Google Artifact Repository, Azure Container Registry etc., Once you identified the image you need and you install docker on you host bringing up the application is as easy as running the command docker run Anisble, docker run mongodb if you need to run multiple instance of the web service you simply add as many instances as you need"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Image: An Image is a readonly, package or a template, It is used to create one or more containers, Containers are running instances of images that are isolated and have their own environments and set of processes"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Docker in DevOps"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Traditionally, Developers develop applications, then they hand it over to the operations team to deploy and manage it in the production environment along with set of information, information about how the host must be setup, what prerequisites are to be installed on the host, how the dependencies are to be configured. Since the ops team did not really develop the application on their own they struggled with setting it up, when they had an issue they work with develpers to resolve. With Docker the developers and operations team work hand-in-hand to transform the guide into a docker file with both of their requirements the docker file is then used to create a docker image for their application This image can now run on any host with Docker installed on it and it's guarenteed to run the same everywhere."
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
